# ── Stationly session maintenance ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Install with:  crontab ~/stationly-backend/ops/maintenance.crontab
# Inspect with:  crontab -l
#
# Two nightly jobs. Spec: stationly-backend/docs/HANDOVER_SESSION_SYNC.md §3.1;
# design rationale: StationlyUI/docs/DESIGN_SESSIONS_AND_SYNC.md §9.
#
#   sweep     DISABLED, and its line below is commented out. It releases the
#             subscription holds held by accounts whose every session has aged
#             past the 90-day TTL — but that TTL is measured on `lastSeen`,
#             which the shipped Android build only refreshes on an explicit
#             sign-in, so it currently reads "has not SIGNED IN for 90 days"
#             and would release users who open the app every day. The job is
#             also gated in code by SWEEP_ENABLED in
#             src/services/sessionMaintenanceService.ts — read that comment
#             before uncommenting anything here; the cron line alone will not
#             turn it back on.
#   reconcile recomputes station_subscriptions from the live accounts and
#             repairs the drift left behind when a post-transaction registry
#             write is lost to a process restart. It ALSO rebuilds the
#             `user_watch` push-audience index on the same pass — that table has
#             no cache-miss path (an empty one is indistinguishable from "nobody
#             watches this line"), so a nightly repair is the only thing standing
#             between a lost SQLite file and a silently dead disruption push.
#
# Both are idempotent. A retry, an overlapping run, and a manual invocation
# during an incident are all harmless.
#
# ## Why a crontab and not a timer inside the app
# staging starts the backend with `pm2 start -i max`, i.e. cluster mode. An
# in-process nightly timer would fire once per worker, and a registry recompute
# racing itself across N workers is exactly the contention the transactions
# exist to serialise. One crontab line fires once no matter how many workers
# are listening. (Prod runs `-i 1` today, but the reasoning should not depend
# on that staying true.)
#
# ## Why the wrapper rather than a bare curl
# It keeps the secret out of this file and out of `ps`; see the header of
# maintenance_cron.sh. Rotating LIVESTREAM_INGEST_SECRET needs no edit here.
#
# ## Times are UTC
# The host runs Etc/UTC, so these fire at 04:00 / 04:20 London during BST and
# 03:00 / 03:20 during GMT. Both sit inside the overnight quiet window either
# way, so the drift is not worth a timezone dependency.
#
# The 20-minute gap is headroom for reconcile's "let Pass 1's own writes
# settle" margin plus a slow sweep. It is generous, not measured. Reconcile
# keeps its 03:20 slot while sweep is disabled so the schedule does not have to
# be re-proved when sweep returns.

SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin

# DISABLED — see the sweep note in the header. Do not uncomment without also
# flipping SWEEP_ENABLED, which would otherwise make this a nightly no-op.
# 0  3 * * *  /home/ubuntu/stationly-backend/ops/maintenance_cron.sh sweep
20 3 * * *  /home/ubuntu/stationly-backend/ops/maintenance_cron.sh reconcile
